INT. FUNERAL HOME EMBALMING ROOM - NIGHT

Tile. Drains. One steel table, covered. A radio hums static in the corner.

DELPHINE ROUX (30s, mortician's apron) checks a clipboard. Her assistant, OSEI BANDELE (20s, headphones around his neck), eats crackers from the sleeve. At the door, WALT KOWALCZYK (60s, groundskeeper, flashlight), keeps his back to the wall.

She frowns. The clipboard lists one body. The table is lumpy under the sheet, wrong somehow.

DELPHINE
Osei. How many did we take in?

OSEI
One. Mr. Alder.

DELPHINE
Then why is it that big?

He stops chewing. Walt's flashlight beam trembles on the floor.

WALT
Don't lift it.

DELPHINE
Walt.

WALT
Don't. Please.

She lifts it anyway. Underneath lies old MR. ALDER, gray, still. And on his chest, curled like a cat, sits a girl of about eight in a wet nightgown, eyes open, humming along with the radio.

DELPHINE
Sweetheart, whose are you?

OSEI
^Oh no. No no no.

The girl stops humming. Looks at her. Smiles with all her teeth.

MOTHER ALDER (O.S.)
(from the hallway)
Marnie. Come away from there.

The lights flicker. Walt drops the flashlight. It rolls to the drain and goes out.

They back toward the door. He grabs the knob. It's already turning from the other side.

DEACON FIRMIN (O.S.)
Open up. Please. She's followed me home too.
